      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we can first calculate the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) and observe its pattern.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\)\nWe can do this by performing the division \\( 1 \\div 7 \\).\n\n\\[\n1 \\div 7 = 0.\\overline{142857}\n\\]\n\nThe decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is a repeating decimal with the repeating block \"142857\". This means that the digits \"142857\" repeat indefinitely.\n\n### Step 2: Identify the length of the repeating block\nThe repeating block \"142857\" has a length of 6 digits.\n\n### Step 3: Determine the position of the 100th digit\nTo find the 100th digit, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating block. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6.\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThe remainder is 4, which means that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating block \"142857\".\n\n### Step 4: Identify the 4th digit in the repeating block\nThe repeating block is \"142857\". The 4th digit in this block is \"2\".\n\n### Conclusion\nThe 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
